#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179456 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb008515-d668-509b-8c8a-76e892e709cc" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "692dbdea-922d-568c-bc00-4972dd733ca9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6305" :question/problem "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = 2x+y^2$ along the quarter-circle $x^2+y^2=4,$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-2,0)$ in the second quadrant." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/d0ae53dd5d0932cbf834a166e851c227f75cb546b591a9fd43930ec06f0e9ddf.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = 2x+y^2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179502 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca6cfe72-a82e-59be-b454-bcc643b3596c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "3fe61e6a-ce68-5f85-98f3-e4af5a39de5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9438" :question/problem "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = x^2-1,$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-1 \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t-1)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t-1\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179509 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c33b34e-5b57-51c1-91d0-e098364bcdfb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "a4c10c26-84c5-5ec7-9468-93986e27f492" :question/math-academy-id "e-9462" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y) =x^2-2$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 4.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2-2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t-2 \\\\  & =2(2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t,$ $\\textrm d u=2\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{0}^{4\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{4\\pi } \\\\  & =2(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c33b34e-5b57-51c1-91d0-e098364bcdfb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e96ff12c-2c07-5226-bcac-0a5b1d125519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177329} {:answer/id #uuid "030bb83b-fb03-5eb2-a457-86fde49820ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177330} {:answer/id #uuid "1f658fd4-210e-50bf-8fe1-0728d8f5028b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177331} {:answer/id #uuid "33e7b421-1159-5086-9ca7-12c054916050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177332} {:answer/id #uuid "47ef803b-ccc2-57cc-bb4f-1ceafaef8c08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f658fd4-210e-50bf-8fe1-0728d8f5028b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177331} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e70b79a-0ae0-5af2-9719-027724a6cf99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b79282b0-117d-50c1-87eb-8d6109e91a7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122136" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =16{cos}^{2}⁡t-16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =16({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t,$ $\\text{d}u=2\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =32{\\int }_{0}^{4\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =32\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{4\\pi } \\\\  & =32(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca6cfe72-a82e-59be-b454-bcc643b3596c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c24c262-4398-5446-bc9d-27645b4f0cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{0}^{2π}(2\\cos t + \\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177341} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c38c61-4b53-5b3b-9117-947ce6775169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2∫_{0}^{2π}(\\cos t + 2\\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177342} {:answer/id #uuid "de07f689-6383-5efe-90dd-91b2b4623e80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{0}^{2π}(\\cos t + 2\\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177343} {:answer/id #uuid "0eaf656d-f304-5384-9efa-03f1f9909af8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2∫_{0}^{2π}(2\\cos t + \\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177344} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce387ae-f4c2-5ae9-a54a-6936b3b4fe44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{0}^{2π}(\\cos^{2} t + 2\\sin t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de07f689-6383-5efe-90dd-91b2b4623e80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{0}^{2π}(\\cos t + 2\\sin^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177343} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f90318c-819f-5544-b17a-bb82ed972697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8f5c163-ed26-54b5-8aab-82f3a498034d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122263" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c33b34e-5b57-51c1-91d0-e098364bcdfb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8145b74b-be63-5a9f-a264-3f59798f21bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334790} {:answer/id #uuid "fc77084d-14fe-54cb-b004-572d952a2b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{e}^{4}-4}{{e}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334791} {:answer/id #uuid "80f0cb5c-3602-5ae9-9316-803822220bd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+4{e}^{7}}{{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334792} {:answer/id #uuid "1fd9e411-b4d1-5465-9536-c9d3f377fd10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334793} {:answer/id #uuid "66be6060-80cf-5421-9e4f-71adb440f026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{4}}{{e}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc77084d-14fe-54cb-b004-572d952a2b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{e}^{4}-4}{{e}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334791} :answer-field/id #uuid "01356ccf-ffaa-5d0b-b847-2a2b4c167823" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86c4e485-7c50-5624-b574-a3d3f04e4c55" :question/math-academy-id "q-122441" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/e27774d260c053a7835b30e62c2d4b41be4b8dc22efcba8f7aa95704590561cf.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y-3}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as  \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y-3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3}\\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3,$ $\\text{d}u=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3}\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{-7}^{-3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\,{e}^{u}{|}_{-7}^{-3} \\\\  & =4({e}^{-3}-{e}^{-7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{e}^{3}}-\\frac{4}{{e}^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{4}-4}{{e}^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca6cfe72-a82e-59be-b454-bcc643b3596c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "084d8872-827a-55b1-b161-c7d18688c0e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334797} {:answer/id #uuid "e23a577e-9028-5aa8-9023-5c65c50720fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334798} {:answer/id #uuid "444677cb-94c0-5055-a2b3-a760b36c18b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334799} {:answer/id #uuid "9438e5a2-745c-547d-9d38-231d3a3521a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334800} {:answer/id #uuid "b63ec3f9-6f0f-59b2-bf1a-ec585fabb91f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "444677cb-94c0-5055-a2b3-a760b36c18b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334799} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9ca0824-4056-5b7d-9bb7-be2b16b38775" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8954060-eb74-5d21-b7ff-5405a3086ea5" :question/math-academy-id "q-122489" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=3-{y}^{2},$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3-(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =3-9{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3-9{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca6cfe72-a82e-59be-b454-bcc643b3596c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbb72288-83f4-59ac-9ad4-8945758a6ea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334804} {:answer/id #uuid "a255bb03-b6aa-58ea-9b20-ee91c04fc360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-5)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334805} {:answer/id #uuid "abba55df-8ca9-525d-9810-ee4726b27692" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334806} {:answer/id #uuid "78ff4fbd-185e-576a-b1fd-f5b900404988" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334807} {:answer/id #uuid "77285a09-7234-5603-bee4-f071b99ce370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-5)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abba55df-8ca9-525d-9810-ee4726b27692" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334806} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb1cba96-120c-54ae-b108-697966842f8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "768f5ade-57c0-528d-9864-4b38b9f72d0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-122504" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy,$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=25?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $5$ centered at $(-1,0).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡t+25{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca6cfe72-a82e-59be-b454-bcc643b3596c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "502f7bd3-b3b8-59ab-8cb3-1b60d076dd5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334811} {:answer/id #uuid "0377f728-ce56-5c1d-b7f6-836f6d6dc584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334812} {:answer/id #uuid "4e48e65c-224d-592c-9bfc-a6bf22f068b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334813} {:answer/id #uuid "660bd6d0-6a5a-5c88-9858-c77fcb9805ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334814} {:answer/id #uuid "123894c1-ef6d-570e-8499-2dd61182c0b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0377f728-ce56-5c1d-b7f6-836f6d6dc584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334812} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a7b34be-b7be-5575-8b12-756b51a4f57c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01acc2c4-61a4-5484-813b-8353dc8736ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-122509" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={y}^{3},$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=36?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $6$ centered at $(1,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)j \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡t+36{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={y}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }((6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)^{3})\\cdot 6\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca6cfe72-a82e-59be-b454-bcc643b3596c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c93b67b4-3a94-5fea-91c6-6ed2c229336c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334818} {:answer/id #uuid "41452db0-144d-5b4c-8eec-4472970e5d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334819} {:answer/id #uuid "f008596e-0d30-51c8-bd22-e08bb4e20a6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334820} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f14c2f-6d6d-5b0b-b7e7-13ae02df4148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334821} {:answer/id #uuid "a09be9c1-a58b-525d-aa2c-541560d9d069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41452db0-144d-5b4c-8eec-4472970e5d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334819} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c6514f7-ea9e-53f5-89fc-8fff62878658" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c026636-3333-5149-910a-9646132297e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-122540" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1-{y}^{2},$ traversed once along the circle $C$ with equation $(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at $(-3,0).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb008515-d668-509b-8c8a-76e892e709cc" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5848978-19e0-51bc-8df2-10f1b94c76da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334825} {:answer/id #uuid "66dde6ac-a53e-5679-b41d-b70b065a8cce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334826} {:answer/id #uuid "0039ae3d-b23f-55bd-9e0a-93be7ddfc87e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334827} {:answer/id #uuid "6c2f8ed3-4291-5e57-a0b9-25958e4fec76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334828} {:answer/id #uuid "4348f031-06e0-5b76-a5c1-9bf9472a3ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66dde6ac-a53e-5679-b41d-b70b065a8cce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334826} :answer-field/id #uuid "f79a95e5-9407-5002-ae5d-d6dcc03c4e27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "823c89b1-3eae-5e6a-ab33-4fed7bd74bba" :question/math-academy-id "q-122568" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/761643a777650909b8c7ea5477b4fe246ac4032fe183df5d9ad5cb5e08ac1ecc.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+y$ along a circle of radius $4,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb008515-d668-509b-8c8a-76e892e709cc" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55daff39-4782-54dd-9c47-d43149c24a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334832} {:answer/id #uuid "96054743-b3fd-5c1f-8313-f480a9f08b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334833} {:answer/id #uuid "56b5526e-8522-5782-9964-ea88b6402f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334834} {:answer/id #uuid "f4afc315-64fd-57bb-b795-ba0df6a580ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334835} {:answer/id #uuid "ba040e42-4fc3-5eee-b9ca-3f56113ce05e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4afc315-64fd-57bb-b795-ba0df6a580ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334835} :answer-field/id #uuid "24181d15-5737-5179-bb20-36e6d62ccbef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e173157-70f2-508d-996b-8f564a55490d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122570" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/4403bd230338dee226429d3f8f3ac8eaede1152aff0b6b18bd2e7ad9f3a3b8e3.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=9-{x}^{2}$ along a circle of radius $3,$ traversed from the point $(3,0)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9-{x}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9-(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =9-9{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =9{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb008515-d668-509b-8c8a-76e892e709cc" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15271314-32fe-5ce8-8e4e-3a79f99d8ef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334839} {:answer/id #uuid "025093c9-a121-5fbe-a441-016d472e79ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334840} {:answer/id #uuid "4f378851-4d40-58d8-a942-b49ce7820016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334841} {:answer/id #uuid "79d98dd6-9602-563a-a4f5-a70ae1cb32c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334842} {:answer/id #uuid "7a427321-f760-5ec1-abe2-29526339598f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "025093c9-a121-5fbe-a441-016d472e79ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334840} :answer-field/id #uuid "931bf621-2168-5426-b433-28f80891e25b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6c2e787-d774-546b-90c4-c7c2c369feb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-122578" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25,$ traversed from the point $(-5,0)$ to the point $(0,-5)$ in the third quadrant?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $5$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/2ec6f01ab00c17d4888dfefb37c2813139af9c412e99acd1a38680274b0f0ddd.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡t+25{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & ={e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{e}^{5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c33b34e-5b57-51c1-91d0-e098364bcdfb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "860c3d22-ce0e-591a-b810-a7678e5e1f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334846} {:answer/id #uuid "ede80027-e3d1-5048-af45-cefad51b07fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334847} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4275f8-1e72-5fdd-9545-500049bf6a8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334848} {:answer/id #uuid "076c1023-f586-53ab-aa93-9151d67fa434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334849} {:answer/id #uuid "61d88edb-f7f8-599c-a4bf-eaf7c9d0ff0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ede80027-e3d1-5048-af45-cefad51b07fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334847} :answer-field/id #uuid "3403f501-10ad-5e1c-a0a7-52eb157be6e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "45c42586-3a9b-55d5-9072-f51b8fc20108" :question/math-academy-id "q-122580" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=y+1$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $5$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡t+25{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y+1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\,\\text{d}t & =5[-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =5[(-5+5)+(2\\pi -0)] \\\\  & =10\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb008515-d668-509b-8c8a-76e892e709cc" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bedd7e21-89b1-52a8-a4e9-bc2554afcd2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-6{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334853} {:answer/id #uuid "23609e4f-d873-564d-bf17-3c99fc6f4b40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334854} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe70f81-6ec8-5232-af9c-33076eeeb7bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334855} {:answer/id #uuid "a702395e-05a8-5eed-826a-662c54705c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334856} {:answer/id #uuid "21e2af40-ad8f-5c20-9564-191bf02f0bb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21e2af40-ad8f-5c20-9564-191bf02f0bb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334857} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e83a7f7-25de-527c-ad06-4fd140ddda37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b50e6e3-88d1-56d2-a993-e2318e36e7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-122582" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\,{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36,$ traversed from the point $(6,0)$ to the point $(0,6)$ in the first quadrant?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $6$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/c1e3332017b795ed6733a0547e6e449fdba3874f1d1e755118a0687ccaf7448c.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡t+36{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\,{e}^{1-{y}^{2}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =36{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{1-36{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c33b34e-5b57-51c1-91d0-e098364bcdfb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ed1aa1-827e-5b21-974a-2c510a7002ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334860} {:answer/id #uuid "a85c214d-5b89-5302-b4f3-c25f9c5d606d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334861} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9bc228-688e-56b4-a722-b4ddf5775114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334862} {:answer/id #uuid "dd6a74ff-a3ee-5633-984c-54de9b8d0df3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334863} {:answer/id #uuid "83c333ce-330d-5de1-ba83-1f6ceb5dbbc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83c333ce-330d-5de1-ba83-1f6ceb5dbbc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334864} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3ceaec2-92be-5d39-9923-d9a1b336e7e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c336521-37e1-5552-8b76-3afe0c9e8b61" :question/math-academy-id "q-122585" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=9{sin}^{2}⁡t+1,$ $\\text{d}u=18\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{1}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb008515-d668-509b-8c8a-76e892e709cc" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over a Part-Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da018880-d2bc-5549-8b6a-0e41c79c0618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334867} {:answer/id #uuid "a60a5289-61a7-50d5-8e40-5027ae35eb80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334868} {:answer/id #uuid "67fa64e9-4a7d-5630-b1aa-c4ec7ca9c486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334869} {:answer/id #uuid "852a4ed4-cb6b-5412-8af9-f822fcd3f67d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334870} {:answer/id #uuid "b778b2e0-185b-53af-94cc-88bdbfacdfd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a60a5289-61a7-50d5-8e40-5027ae35eb80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334868} :answer-field/id #uuid "271c59af-7cac-58cd-9f46-dc748c9bfe99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b42e9373-4893-5f93-a4eb-1c63f18b5e00" :question/math-academy-id "q-122588" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\,\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,-3)$ to the point $(3,0)$ in the fourth quadrant?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/b7c2c6f9e50988629007a06213cb3acf0a48f9951f026fb70d5a986b760abdfe.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\,\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{9-(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{9-9{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }9{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c33b34e-5b57-51c1-91d0-e098364bcdfb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Line Integral of a Function Over a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9623430a-8496-5359-946c-836d1db59540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 - e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178560} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5de4cb-c5af-53b2-bd49-da848c222d7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178561} {:answer/id #uuid "55e07cd7-b8e6-5330-9f9f-8abdbb1229a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - \\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178562} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec0d12c-d0d8-58e9-8af9-0b9451a33bd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178563} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca3d6fe-fbc3-5944-b4d8-4e57f0f687ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e^{2}} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ca3d6fe-fbc3-5944-b4d8-4e57f0f687ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e^{2}} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178564} :answer-field/id #uuid "665543e2-02cf-50c4-8def-352da6219351" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce49b8d5-a7c7-525b-89a9-21901c00319a" :question/math-academy-id "q-40255" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057418/assets/cbd92b205693f1056e4dc2d0fc8154843e62b433b66d9b0ba8918d7f5bb3b8bf.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(-2,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{0}^{-2}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{-2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
